# APGO + NGINX Proxy Manager on Kubernetes, in the "apgo" namespace. APGO
# images come from your registry; NPM comes from Docker Hub.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE POD, ONE NETWORK NAMESPACE — and why that is the whole design
#
# The rule that governs everything here: a listener is reachable over the
# overlay only if it lives in the SAME network namespace as the ovl0 TUN.
#
# There are two constraints on this stack, and they used to point in opposite
# directions:
#
#   1. NPM's admin UI on :81 must share a namespace with the TUN, or overlay
#      traffic can only reach it by crossing a namespace boundary.
#   2. NPM's Node backend on :3000 must NOT be in the host namespace, or another
#      service on the node (Grafana et al) steals the port, the backend
#      crash-loops, nginx serves the UI but 502s every /api call, and the
#      dashboard reports "API is not healthy" forever.
#
# The previous layout ran apgo with hostNetwork (TUN in the HOST namespace) and
# NPM as a separate pod (:3000 safely private). That satisfies #2 and BREAKS #1:
# reaching :81 then required a CNI portmap hostPort DNAT plus a forward across
# the CNI bridge. That path is fragile for packets arriving on a TUN, and even
# when it forwards it breaks on MSS — NPM's socket only saw eth0's ~1450 MTU and
# advertised a segment size too large for the overlay, so the handshake
# succeeded and the first full-size response was silently dropped. Loopback
# worked because portmap has a special lo path with a 65k MTU, which is exactly
# the "127.0.0.1:81 answers, every other address hangs" symptom.
#
# A single NON-hostNetwork pod satisfies BOTH constraints at once. The pod's
# namespace is private, so :3000 cannot collide with anything on the node; and
# the overlay client creates ovl0 INSIDE that same namespace, so NPM's :81 and
# the admin dashboard's :8789 are plain local sockets on the overlay address. An
# inbound packet for 10.22.22.22:81 is delivered straight to a socket — no DNAT,
# no forwarding, no MTU renegotiation, no proxy hop.
#
# This is the same shape the zerotier-self stack uses (podman containers with
# --network host inside a pod that is itself NOT hostNetwork), which is why that
# stack can serve NPM at <overlay-ip>:81 with no special plumbing at all.
#
# What this deleted, relative to the previous version: the separate npm
# Deployment, the npm-admin Service, the npm-admin-proxy socat Deployment, the
# TCPMSS clamp initContainer, and the podAffinity gluing npm to apgo. None of
# them were wrong; they were all patches over the namespace split.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHAT IS AND IS NOT PUBLISHED ON THE NODE
#
#   80/443 TCP  — hostPort. Public ingress arriving on the node's physical NIC.
#                 This is ordinary north-south traffic and the portmap path
#                 handles it fine; it is only the TUN-ingress case that didn't.
#   6970  UDP   — hostPort. The overlay transport. A pod namespace has no
#                 host-facing UDP port of its own, so without this the client
#                 could dial out but nothing could punch back in.
#   81    TCP   — NOT published. Deliberately. It is reachable at
#                 10.22.22.22:81 over the overlay, which is how you use it.
#   8789  TCP   — NOT published. Same: 10.22.22.22:8789 over the overlay.
#
# Note that 10.22.22.22 is now a POD address rather than a node address. For
# tunnelling in from a laptop over APGO that changes nothing — you still dial
# 10.22.22.22:81 and :8789. But the NODE itself no longer has a route into the
# overlay, so nothing running directly on zx3t/zx7t can reach 10.22.22.x. If you
# ever need that back, run a second small hostNetwork APGO client with its own
# overlay address rather than reverting this file.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHY THIS LAYOUT AND NOT hostNetwork — THE REASON THAT ACTUALLY MATTERS
#
# The pod namespace ISOLATES ovl0, and that is the point.
#
# These nodes already run a SECOND APGO client (the podman one, tun.name ovl7),
# and it is where the Kubernetes node IPs come from: zx3t is 10.22.22.3 and zx7t
# is 10.22.22.7 — both inside overlay_cidr 10.22.22.0/24. If this client's ovl0
# is ALSO in the host namespace, that namespace ends up with two connected routes
# for 10.22.22.0/24 and the kernel picks between them arbitrarily. Replies to an
# overlay peer then leave via the wrong tunnel under the wrong node identity, and
# you get: ~66% ping loss, endless "cipher: message authentication failed" and
# "replayed or expired nonce", constant re-handshakes, and 10.22.22.22:81 timing
# out even from the node holding it. All of that was measured, not theorised.
#
# Running the client in this pod's own namespace puts ovl0 in a routing table
# where nothing else claims that /24. Same reason the zerotier-self stack works:
# its ZeroTier TUN is alone in ITS pod namespace too.
#
# THE COST THIS LAYOUT USED TO HAVE, AND HOW IT IS PAID OFF NOW
#
# Outbound overlay packets leave via the CNI bridge, which masquerades them and
# REWRITES THE SOURCE PORT — a different external port per destination. That is
# a symmetric NAT stacked under the router's, and it defeats hole punching, so
# sessions to NAT'd peers (phones especially) fell back to RELAY. That single
# side effect is what drove the repeated attempts to move back to hostNetwork.
#
# It is fixed HERE instead, by the apgo-natpin DaemonSet at the bottom of this
# file. One rule per node, ahead of the CNI masquerade:
#
#   -t nat -I POSTROUTING -p udp -s <pod cidr> --sport 6970 \
#          -j MASQUERADE --to-ports 6970
#
# --to-ports forces a PORT-PRESERVING, endpoint-independent mapping for the
# overlay's UDP: every peer now observes the same <node public ip>:6970, which
# is precisely the property hole punching needs, and it is the same property
# hostNetwork was being used to obtain. Inbound is already deterministic via
# the hostPort DNAT below. So you get direct sessions AND a private namespace.
#
# DO NOT "fix" relaying by moving back to hostNetwork. That trades a working
# path for the ovl0/ovl7 route collision above — and, as of Aug 18, for a
# second failure this file had not seen before: with the TUN in the host
# namespace, the kubelet's probe target (status.hostIP, an OVERLAY address on
# this cluster) stops being locally deliverable when the two clients disagree
# about who owns 10.22.22.0/24. NPM then answers instantly on 127.0.0.1:81 and
# times out on 10.22.22.3:81, the startupProbe never passes, and the pod sits
# at 2/3 Running with 0 restarts forever. The probes below are exec-based
# specifically so that a routing fault can never again present as a dead pod.
#
# If direct sessions still degrade after this, the next levers are an anchor —
# a publicly reachable endpoint in static_peers on every node — or
# RENDEZVOUS_SERVERS below, both of which are additive to the port pin.
#
# PRECONDITION, on each ingress node:  sudo ufw allow 6970/udp
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECRETS — create these once (network name + PSK, and the dashboard login):
#
# kubectl create namespace apgo
# kubectl -n apgo delete secret apgo-network --ignore-not-found
# kubectl -n apgo create secret generic apgo-network \
#   --from-literal=NETWORK_NAME='network-name' \
#   --from-literal=PSK='base64:PSK'
# kubectl -n apgo rollout restart deploy/apgo
#
# # APGO admin dashboard login.
# kubectl -n apgo create secret generic apgo-admin \
#   --from-literal=ADMIN_USER='admin' \
#   --from-literal=ADMIN_PASSWORD='pass'
# kubectl -n apgo rollout restart deploy/apgo
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ROLLING OUT A NEW BUILD — read this before wondering why your change is not live
#
# The admin dashboard's HTML is COMPILED INTO the apgoadmin binary (//go:embed
# static in admin/main.go). Editing admin/static/index.html on your workstation
# changes nothing in the cluster until you rebuild and push the image. There is
# no volume mount to shortcut it, by design: the dashboard and the binary that
# serves it are versioned together.
#
# And `kubectl apply -f apgo.yaml` alone will NOT pick up a new image either,
# for two compounding reasons:
#
#   1. The image reference (localhost:5000/apgo-admin:latest) is unchanged, so
#      the Deployment's pod template is unchanged, so Kubernetes sees nothing
#      to roll out and does nothing at all.
#   2. Even forcing a restart is not enough: these containers run with
#      imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent, so the kubelet reuses the :latest image
#      already cached on the node and never contacts the registry. A moving
#      tag plus IfNotPresent means "pin to whatever this node happened to pull
#      first", which is almost never what you want during development.
#
# So the full sequence, from an edited file to a live dashboard:
#
#   # 1. build + push (from the repo root, on a node that can reach the registry)
#   podman build -t localhost:5000/apgo-admin:latest -f apgo-admin.containerfile .
#   podman push  localhost:5000/apgo-admin:latest
#
#   # 2. make the node forget the cached copy, or the push above is invisible
#   #    to it. On k3s/containerd:
#   sudo crictl rmi localhost:5000/apgo-admin:latest
#   #    (Docker: docker rmi localhost:5000/apgo-admin:latest)
#
#   # 3. restart so the kubelet pulls again
#   kubectl -n apgo rollout restart deploy/apgo
#   kubectl -n apgo rollout status  deploy/apgo
#
# To CONFIRM you are running what you think you are — worth doing once, because
# a stale dashboard looks exactly like a change that did not work:
#
#   kubectl -n apgo get pod -l app=apgo \
#     -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.containerStatuses[*].imageID}{"\n"}'
#
# Compare that digest before and after. If it did not change, step 2 was
# skipped and you are still on the old build.
#
# If you iterate on the dashboard often, the friction is worth removing
# properly: tag each build (…:2026-09-02-1) and bump the tag here, so the
# Deployment actually changes and Kubernetes rolls it out on its own. Immutable
# tags are the real fix; steps 2 and 3 are the workaround for a moving one.
# Alternatively set imagePullPolicy: Always on the apgo* containers — but note
# the trade-off the comment at overlay-client describes: a node whose registry
# is unreachable then cannot restart the overlay AT ALL, which is a bad state
# for the software you may be relying on to reach that cluster.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MIGRATING FROM THE TWO-DEPLOYMENT LAYOUT
#
# The old objects are not in this file any more, so `kubectl apply` will not
# remove them. Delete them explicitly, once:
#
#   kubectl -n apgo delete deploy/npm deploy/npm-admin-proxy
#   kubectl -n apgo delete svc/npm-admin
#   kubectl apply -f apgo.yaml
#
# Delete npm FIRST: it holds npm-data and npm-letsencrypt, which are
# ReadWriteOnce, and the new apgo pod cannot attach them until it lets go.
#
# The MSS clamp installed by the old initContainer persists in the node's mangle
# table after the pod is gone. It is harmless, but to clean up:
#   sudo iptables -t mangle -D FORWARD -o ovl+ -p tcp --tcp-flags SYN,RST SYN \
#     -j TCPMSS --clamp-mss-to-pmtu
#
# Any NPM proxy host pointing at apgo-admin.apgo.svc:8788 can go back to
# 127.0.0.1:8789 — the dashboard is a container in this same pod again.

apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: apgo
  labels:
    name: apgo
---
# High scheduling priority so a rescheduled apgo pod jumps ahead of ordinary
# workloads (and can preempt them) when a node comes back or fails over.
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: apgo-critical
value: 1000000
globalDefault: false
description: "APGO overlay + ingress stack — reschedule before ordinary workloads."
---
# =============================================================================
# STORAGE CLASS: linstor-apgo-r2 — DEFINED IN piraeus-linstor.yaml, NOT HERE
#
# MOVED 2026-09-04. This file used to declare linstor-apgo-r2 itself. So did
# three other repos for their own classes, and the four copies had already
# drifted: different placement counts, different split-brain policy, and
# allowRemoteVolumeAccess unset in every one of them — which is what let a pod
# be scheduled onto a node holding none of its data.
#
# Storage policy now lives with the storage platform. This file declares its
# PVCs and references the class BY NAME; it does not define it.
#
# apgo-state / npm-data / npm-letsencrypt uses linstor-apgo-r2:
#   placementCount 4 — one replica on every node, so the pod's fallback scheduling is real
#   allowRemoteVolumeAccess: "false" — the CSI driver reports only nodes that
#   hold a replica, so the scheduler will not place this pod anywhere its data
#   is not. That is what makes the nodeAffinity below a safety net rather than
#   the only thing standing between you and a pod stuck in Init forever.
#
# THIS FILE NO LONGER STANDS ALONE FOR STORAGE. On a fresh cluster apply
# piraeus-linstor.yaml FIRST. Without it the PVC below stays Pending with
# "storageclass.storage.k8s.io not found" — which is the correct failure, and
# is not the same as the PVC hanging Pending because a placement constraint
# matched zero nodes. Check `kubectl get sc` before debugging anything else.
#
# TO CHANGE THE CLASS: edit piraeus-linstor.yaml. StorageClass.parameters is
# immutable, so it is delete-then-apply, and it changes NOTHING about volumes
# that already exist. That file documents the migration steps.
# =============================================================================
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  # The overlay node's IDENTITY lives here (node.key + admin material) — this
  # PVC following the pod between zx3t/zx7t is what makes failover come back
  # as the same overlay node at the same 10.22.22.22 address.
  name: apgo-state
  namespace: apgo
spec:
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 256Mi
  storageClassName: linstor-apgo-r2
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: npm-data
  namespace: apgo
spec:
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi
  storageClassName: linstor-apgo-r2
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: npm-letsencrypt
  namespace: apgo
spec:
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 512Mi
  storageClassName: linstor-apgo-r2
---
# =============================================================================
# 2026-10-18: THE POD NOW USES THE *-v2 CLAIMS BELOW, NOT THE THREE ABOVE.
#
# The original three volumes kept their only UpToDate replicas on zx3t/zx7t.
# When both remote sites dropped off (etcd/WAN trouble), no reachable node had
# a usable copy, every attach failed with "could not determine device path",
# and apgo sat in Init:0/1 for hours. The data was recovered from zx7t's
# backing files and restored into these v2 claims on home-replicated, which
# keeps both replicas on the two LOCAL nodes (zx11t + zx13t) and does NOT
# allow remote (diskless) access. So:
#   * the pod can only run on zx11t or zx13t (see the required affinity)
#   * a remote-site outage can no longer take apgo's data away
# The three claims above are kept as the fallback copy of the old data. Delete
# them (and their PVs) only once the v2 volumes are confirmed good.
# =============================================================================
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: apgo-state-v2
  namespace: apgo
spec:
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 256Mi
  storageClassName: home-replicated
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: npm-data-v2
  namespace: apgo
spec:
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi
  storageClassName: home-replicated
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: npm-letsencrypt-v2
  namespace: apgo
spec:
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 512Mi
  storageClassName: home-replicated
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: apgo-config
  namespace: apgo
data:
  OVERLAY_CIDR: "10.22.22.0/24"
  FRIENDLY_NAME: "k8s-node"
  # Pin this node's overlay IP (matches the admin-panel assignment). Without a
  # pin the client auto-derives an IP from its key, which can collide with
  # another node's (that exact collision — two nodes on 10.22.22.105 — caused
  # heavy packet loss here once). Keep this in sync if you re-assign it.
  OVERLAY_ADDRESS: "10.22.22.22"
  # Set to "1" to make this node an internet EXIT / outproxy.
  #
  # NOTE: exit/outproxy from a pod namespace NATs twice — once by the client's
  # own iptables rules inside the pod, then again by the CNI masquerade on the
  # way out of the node. It works, but it is a slower path than it was under
  # hostNetwork. If you turn this on and it underperforms, that is why.
  EXIT_NODE: "1"
  # OPTIONAL nodeName -> PUBLIC IP map. LEAVE IT EMPTY unless you have proven
  # you need it.
  #
  # Normally you do NOT: a pod's traffic egresses via the node it runs on, so
  # STUN reports that node's own public IP, which is exactly where the hostPort
  # DNAT lives — the advertised endpoint is correct with no configuration, and
  # it follows the pod across a reschedule by itself.
  #
  # This exists only for the asymmetric case, where a cluster's egress leaves
  # through a DIFFERENT node than the pod runs on (policy routing, an egress
  # gateway, a default route over a tunnel). There STUN reports the egress
  # node, peers dial a machine with no route to this pod, and everything falls
  # back to relay while the endpoint looks plausible.
  #
  # HOW TO TELL, rather than guess — compare the two:
  #   kubectl -n apgo exec deploy/apgo -c overlay-client -- wget -qO- ip.me   # egress
  #   ssh <node> curl -4 ip.me                                               # the node
  # Same answer  -> leave this empty; STUN is right.
  # Different    -> fill in "<nodeName>=<node public ip>" entries. The
  #                 entrypoint resolves the pod's ACTUAL spec.nodeName, so it
  #                 still follows a reschedule; nothing is pinned.
  NODE_PUBLIC_IPS: ""
  # Optional HTTPS discovery servers for BitTorrent-blocked networks (comma
  # list). Worth populating now: with the client behind CNI masquerade, a
  # rendezvous server is the cleanest fallback if hole punching degrades.
  RENDEZVOUS_SERVERS: ""
  # Base client config (env vars still override name/PSK/CIDR). STUN servers
  # are REQUIRED for internet peers — without them the client can't learn its
  # public endpoint and hole punching fails. A fixed UDP port keeps the NAT
  # mapping stable across restarts and must match the hostPort on the
  # overlay-client container below.
  client.yaml: |
    udp_listen_port: 6970

    # Announce the hostPort, not the port STUN observed.
    #
    # STUN reports the port the CNI masquerade picked for the packet sent TO the
    # STUN server — a different port for every destination, so what this node
    # published to trackers was an endpoint nothing could ever reach. 6970 is
    # DNAT'd to this pod and is stable.
    #
    # As of the apgo-natpin DaemonSet this is no longer just an announcement —
    # it is TRUE. natpin pins the outbound masquerade to 6970 as well, so the
    # port peers observe and the port announced here are the same number, and
    # the mapping is endpoint-independent instead of per-destination.
    #
    # This is ALSO what makes the pod's endpoint failover-safe with nothing
    # pinned: STUN supplies the public IP of whichever node the pod currently
    # runs on, this supplies the port, and the pair is re-derived on every
    # announce (and immediately when the IP changes). Move the pod to another
    # node and the advertised endpoint follows it by itself.
    #
    # Completes the path from outside the site only if inbound UDP 6970 is
    # open on that node (`sudo ufw allow 6970/udp`); otherwise peers reach the
    # pod by relay, which costs a hop but works.
    advertise_port: 6970


    tun:
      # MUST match the rest of the fleet. config/client.yaml in this repo ships
      # mtu: 1280, but this ConfigMap never set it, so this node was silently
      # running the client's built-in default of 1420 (see client/tun_linux.go)
      # while every other node was at 1280. An MTU mismatch produces exactly the
      # "connects, then hangs on the first large response" symptom, and it was
      # doing so independently of any of the namespace problems above.
      mtu: 1280

    # MUST match every node on the network — the podman stack and the
    # macOS/Windows app both use aesgcm; a mismatch fails every handshake
    # with "cipher: message authentication failed".
    cipher: "aesgcm"

    # Symmetric-NAT port prediction. REQUIRED now that this client runs in a pod
    # namespace: CNI masquerade rewrites the source port on every outbound
    # packet, which presents to peers as a symmetric NAT. The client probes the
    # allocation stride and sprays predicted ports during a coordinated punch.
    # config/client.yaml already sets this true for every other node; it was
    # missing here and therefore defaulting to false.
    port_prediction: true

    # Fast discovery tick — the client announces to a rotating subset of
    # trackers each tick (individual trackers still see gentle intervals), so
    # peers converge in seconds instead of the 15-min default.
    min_announce_interval_seconds: 30

    # NAT keepalive (default 10s). Lower it further only for extremely
    # aggressive NATs; sessions self-heal (tear down + re-punch) after ~3
    # missed intervals either way. Keep this SHORT here: the CNI masquerade
    # adds a second conntrack table whose UDP entries also need refreshing.
    keepalive_seconds: 10

    stun_servers:
      - "stun:stun.l.google.com:19302"
      - "stun:stun1.l.google.com:19302"
      - "stun:global.stun.twilio.com:3478"

    # NO static_peers here — deliberately.
    #
    # This used to pin "127.0.0.1:6969" to reach the same-host podman node,
    # because both clients shared the host namespace and so could not discover
    # each other (self-drop on LAN sweep, only one can bind the discovery port,
    # and same-site dial suppression blocked the tracker path). Two things
    # changed:
    #
    #   1. That port was wrong. config/client.yaml has the podman node on
    #      udp_listen_port 32555, not 6969, so this pin has been dialling
    #      nothing.
    #   2. They are no longer in the same namespace, so the reason for a
    #      loopback pin is gone.
    #
    # The pin now lives on the PODMAN side only, where config/client.yaml
    # already has static_peers: ["127.0.0.1:6970"]. That still works: the host's
    # 127.0.0.1:6970 hits this pod through the UDP hostPort below, via portmap's
    # loopback rule. One-directional pinning is enough — the podman node dials,
    # this pod answers, the session is established. See
    # docs/two-nodes-same-host.md.
---
# Custom NGINX snippet appended to every NPM proxy server block. This is the
# ONLY supported way to change proxy behavior in nginx-proxy-manager (the jc21
# image reads /data/nginx/custom/*.conf; there is no env var for it).
# Ref: https://nginxproxymanager.com/advanced-config/#custom-nginx-configurations
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: npm-custom
  namespace: apgo
data:
  server_proxy.conf: |
    # Stream upstreams (RustDesk, long-lived peer connections) straight through
    # instead of buffering, so a slow or aborting client no longer pins an
    # nginx worker waiting out the default 60s proxy timeout. Worker-pool
    # starvation from held connections is what made the admin UI (and every
    # proxy host) hang "after a little while."
    proxy_request_buffering off;
    proxy_buffering off;
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: apgo
  namespace: apgo
  labels:
    app: apgo
spec:
  replicas: 1
  # Recreate: the three PVCs are ReadWriteOnce — a rolling update would deadlock
  # with old and new pods both claiming them. This also guarantees only one
  # holder of the overlay node key exists at a time (see the HA note below).
  strategy:
    type: Recreate
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: apgo
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: apgo
    spec:
      # NO hostNetwork — this is the entire point of this layout. ovl0 is
      # created inside this pod's namespace, alongside NPM's :81 and the admin
      # dashboard's :8789, so all three are local sockets on 10.22.22.22.
      #
      # ClusterFirst (the default) rather than the Default/node resolver this
      # used under hostNetwork. Trade-off: tracker and STUN lookups now go
      # through CoreDNS, so peer discovery has a dependency it did not have
      # before. In exchange, NPM can resolve in-cluster Service names for proxy
      # host backends, which it needs far more often. If cluster DNS here is
      # flaky enough to hurt discovery, switch to dnsPolicy: Default and change
      # any Service-name proxy backends to IPs.
      dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst
      # Fast recovery: jump the scheduling queue, and don't linger 30s in
      # Terminating during a Recreate rollover or node drain.
      priorityClassName: apgo-critical
      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 10
      # HA MODEL — fast-failover ACTIVE-PASSIVE with one shared identity.
      # This pod IS overlay node 10.22.22.22: the node key lives on the
      # apgo-state PVC and OVERLAY_ADDRESS pins the IP, so wherever the pod
      # reschedules it comes back as the SAME node at the SAME overlay address
      # (peers re-handshake to the new endpoint within a few keepalives).
      # Active-ACTIVE (two live replicas sharing the identity) is deliberately
      # NOT done: overlay identity is the node key, and two live holders of one
      # key desync every peer session (endpoint flapping + conflicting
      # announces). Failover between zx3t/zx7t is instead made as fast as
      # possible: hard-pinned to those two nodes, 0s unreachable tolerations,
      # top scheduling priority, and the apgo-prepull DaemonSet keeps images
      # warm on the standby node so recovery never waits on the registry.
      # zx3t/zx7t are control-plane nodes (etcd + apiserver), so they carry the
      # control-plane NoSchedule taint; the toleration below is what lets the
      # pod land on them at all.
      affinity:
        nodeAffinity:
          # ---- REQUIRED: NODES THAT CAN HOLD THIS POD'S DATA ----
          #
          # !! zx5t IS ABSENT FROM THIS LIST ON PURPOSE AND MUST STAY ABSENT. !!
          # It has an hdd-pool but NO DRBD kernel module ("Module drbd not
          # found"), so it can neither hold nor attach a replica.
          #
          # THIS LIST IS NOT A PLACEMENT PREFERENCE. It is the set of nodes on
          # which apgo-state / npm-data / npm-letsencrypt actually have a
          # replica. A node outside it cannot serve this pod AT ALL:
          # linstor-apgo-r2 sets allowRemoteVolumeAccess: "false", so the CSI
          # driver will not attach a diskless resource over the network. The
          # attach does not degrade, it fails:
          #
          #     ControllerPublishVolume failed for pvc-...: 404 Not Found
          #
          # retried forever, pod stuck Init:0/1 with no IP. Observed
          # 2026-09-07: zx3t was fenced during a drill, both remaining ingress
          # nodes reported no free hostPorts, the pod fell through to zx5t
          # under the old `preferred` rule, and the whole overlay stayed down
          # until a human noticed. A pod that cannot mount is not a degraded
          # overlay node — it is an absent one, with a slower failure.
          #
          # WHY THIS IS NOT A RETURN TO THE OLD HARD PIN. The previous hard
          # requirement listed zx3t/zx7t ONLY and produced "0/4 nodes are
          # available" when neither could take the pod. That is what the
          # `preferred` rule was reacting to, and the reaction was right about
          # the problem and wrong about the fix: the answer is a WIDER required
          # set, not an unbounded one. Three data-capable nodes is strictly
          # better fallback than the two the hard pin allowed, and unlike
          # `preferred` every member of the set can actually run the pod.
          #
          # BEFORE ADDING A NODE HERE, it must have BOTH a LINSTOR storage pool
          # AND a replica of all three PVCs. Verify, do not assume:
          #     linstor storage-pool list
          #     linstor resource list | grep -E 'apgo|npm'
          # If zx11t turns out to have no pool, REMOVE IT from this list — a
          # required entry that cannot mount is worse than no entry, because
          # the scheduler will happily use it.
          #
          #
          # ── 2026-10-08: EVERY NODE EXCEPT zx5t ──────────────────────────────
          # Checked 2026-10-08: all three PVs (pvc-9533c01e, pvc-559a3833,
          # pvc-4892bd89) carry volumeAttributes remote-access-policy: "true".
          # They predate linstor-apgo-r2's allowRemoteVolumeAccess "false", and
          # volumeAttributes are immutable, so they KEEP remote access: on a
          # node without a local copy LINSTOR attaches them diskless over the
          # network. Every DRBD node can therefore run this pod. zx5t is the one
          # exception — no DRBD kernel module, so it cannot attach at all (that,
          # not a missing replica, is why the pod hung there on 2026-09-07).
          # The long note above describes the old hand-written node list; it is
          # history. IF THESE PVCs ARE EVER RECREATED under linstor-apgo-r2 they
          # lose remote access and this rule must go back to a node list.
          #
          # ── 2026-10-18: zx11t + zx13t ONLY ──────────────────────────────────
          # The PVCs WERE recreated (apgo-state-v2 / npm-data-v2 /
          # npm-letsencrypt-v2 on home-replicated, see the note at the PVCs).
          # home-replicated has allowRemoteVolumeAccess "false" and keeps its
          # replicas on zx11t + zx13t only, so those are the only two nodes
          # that can mount this pod's data. Any other node here would just
          # hang in Init. The zx7t/zx3t preferences below therefore never
          # match while this rule stands; they are kept for when the remote
          # sites are stable enough to hold replicas again.
          requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
            nodeSelectorTerms:
              - matchExpressions:
                  - key: kubernetes.io/hostname
                    operator: In
                    values: ["zx11t", "zx13t"]
          # ---- PREFERRED: THE INGRESS NODES ----
          #
          # zx3t/zx7t are where this pod belongs: 80/443 are hostPorts and
          # public DNS points at those two boxes. Maximum weight, so the
          # scheduler takes one whenever either can accept the pod.
          #
          # Falling back to zx11t costs north-south ingress only: the overlay
          # stays reachable at 10.22.22.x because EXTRA_CANDIDATES is built
          # from status.hostIP, so whichever node it lands on advertises its
          # own LAN address and peers still get a real endpoint to punch.
          # 80/443 will not serve the public names until DNS points at that
          # box. A degraded overlay node beats an absent one — which is the
          # original reasoning, now applied to a set where every option works.
          #
          # ORDER (2026-10-08): zx7t first, then zx3t, then any other node in
          # the required list. The weights add per node, so zx7t scores 100,
          # zx3t 60, every other node 0. Weight is a strong bias, not an absolute rule:
          # if zx7t cannot take the pod (cordoned, NotReady, hostPort or
          # resources taken) the scheduler falls through to zx3t, then any other node that holds the data.
          preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
            - weight: 100
              preference:
                matchExpressions:
                  - key: kubernetes.io/hostname
                    operator: In
                    values: ["zx7t"]
            - weight: 60
              preference:
                matchExpressions:
                  - key: kubernetes.io/hostname
                    operator: In
                    values: ["zx3t"]
      tolerations:
        # Tolerate the control-plane taint so zx3t / zx7t are schedulable.
        - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoSchedule
        - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/master
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoSchedule
        # Fail over quickly if a node goes away.
        - key: node.kubernetes.io/unreachable
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoExecute
          tolerationSeconds: 0
        - key: node.kubernetes.io/not-ready
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoExecute
          tolerationSeconds: 0
        # Piraeus taints a node when ANY DRBD volume on it loses quorum (e.g.
        # a remote site drops out). That says nothing about apgo's own
        # volumes, so don't let it keep the most important pod off a node.
        - key: drbd.linbit.com/lost-quorum
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoSchedule
        - key: drbd.linbit.com/force-io-error
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoSchedule
      # Copy the custom nginx snippet onto the PVC as a NORMAL writable file.
      # Mounting the ConfigMap directly at /data/nginx/custom/... crash-looped
      # the pod: ConfigMap mounts are always read-only, NPM's s6 "prepare" step
      # chowns everything under /data/nginx, the chown on the mounted file
      # failed, and s6 aborted the container on every start.
      initContainers:
        - name: install-custom-conf
          image: docker.io/jc21/nginx-proxy-manager:latest
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
          command: ["sh", "-c", "mkdir -p /data/nginx/custom && cp /npm-custom/server_proxy.conf /data/nginx/custom/server_proxy.conf"]
          volumeMounts:
            - name: npm-data
              mountPath: /data
            - name: npm-custom
              mountPath: /npm-custom
          resources:
            requests: {cpu: "10m", memory: "32Mi"}
            limits: {cpu: "200m", memory: "128Mi"}

      containers:
        # --- APGO overlay client ---------------------------------------------
        # Creates ovl0 in THIS pod's network namespace. Every other container in
        # this pod shares that namespace, which is what makes NPM reachable on
        # the overlay address with no proxying.
        - name: overlay-client
          image: localhost:5000/apgo-client:latest
          # IfNotPresent: restart from the cached image immediately instead of
          # waiting on (or failing against) the registry. To roll out a new
          # :latest build:  kubectl -n apgo rollout restart deploy/apgo  after
          # pre-pulling, or temporarily flip this back to Always.
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
          securityContext:
            privileged: true
            capabilities:
              add: ["NET_ADMIN"]
          envFrom:
            # optional: if the secret is missing, the client starts in setup mode
            # and you configure it from the admin dashboard (/network-setup).
            - secretRef:
                name: apgo-network
                optional: true
            - configMapRef:
                name: apgo-config
                optional: true
          env:
            # The node's own LAN address, via the downward API.
            #
            # This pod runs in its own network namespace, so the only address it
            # can see is the pod IP (10.42.x.y) — meaningless outside the
            # cluster. The address that actually reaches it is the NODE's LAN
            # address paired with the hostPort below, DNAT'd straight in with no
            # masquerade involved. The pod cannot discover that: the node's
            # interfaces are in a different namespace, and STUN only ever
            # reports whatever the CNI rewrote the source port to.
            #
            # So it has to be told. EXTRA_CANDIDATES is advertised verbatim to
            # peers, ahead of anything STUN-derived. A device on the same LAN
            # then has a real endpoint to punch instead of three dead ones.
            #
            # Kept here because it is genuinely useful diagnostic context in
            # the client log, NOT because it is advertised — see
            # EXTRA_CANDIDATES below for why that is now empty.
            - name: NODE_IP
              valueFrom:
                fieldRef:
                  fieldPath: status.hostIP
            # Which node we landed on — the entrypoint resolves this against
            # NODE_PUBLIC_IPS to advertise the correct public endpoint, and
            # re-resolves it on every reschedule.
            - name: NODE_NAME
              valueFrom:
                fieldRef:
                  fieldPath: spec.nodeName
            - name: NODE_PUBLIC_IPS
              valueFrom:
                configMapKeyRef:
                  name: apgo-config
                  key: NODE_PUBLIC_IPS
                  optional: true
            - name: ADVERTISE_PORT
              value: "6970"
            # EXTRA_CANDIDATES is deliberately EMPTY on this cluster.
            #
            # It used to be "$(NODE_IP):6970", and on a normal cluster that is
            # right: status.hostIP is the node's LAN address, so a peer on that
            # LAN gets a real endpoint to punch. But HERE the k3s node network
            # runs over the APGO overlay, so status.hostIP is an OVERLAY
            # address (zx3t is 10.22.22.3, zx7t is 10.22.22.7 — the podman
            # client's addresses, not this pod's). Advertising one means this
            # pod tells the whole mesh "reach me at another node's overlay IP":
            #
            #   * every dashboard shows this node's endpoint as someone else's
            #     IP, which is simply wrong information;
            #   * desktops reach it by nesting the transport inside the overlay
            #     (works, but an extra encrypt/decrypt hop, and it breaks the
            #     moment that node's client is down);
            #   * PHONES cannot use it at all — a tunnel process's own sockets
            #     bypass its tunnel by OS design — so they stay relayed forever.
            #
            # With it empty, the pod advertises the endpoint it genuinely owns:
            # <this node's public IP, from STUN>:6970. Inbound lands via the
            # hostPort DNAT below; outbound keeps port 6970 because apgo-natpin
            # pins the masquerade. Correct, self-describing, follows the pod
            # across a reschedule, and works for phones and desktops alike.
            #
            # 2026-10-19: still empty here, but the ENTRYPOINT now appends the
            # node's own private LAN addresses, AUTO-DETECTED from the host's
            # interfaces (host /proc mounted at /host/proc, below). That is not
            # the old "$(NODE_IP)" mistake: these are the node's real LAN
            # addresses, not overlay ones, discovered on whatever node the pod
            # lands on. Without them, a device on the same LAN as the pod's node
            # could only use <public ip>:6970, i.e. NAT loopback, which the
            # router does not do - so it was relayed or unreachable.
            # Set LAN_CANDIDATES=off to disable.
            - name: EXTRA_CANDIDATES
              value: ""
            - name: CLIENT_CONFIG
              value: /config/client.yaml
            - name: NODE_KEY_FILE
              value: /state/node.key
            - name: CONTROL_SOCKET
              value: /shared/control.sock
            - name: LOG_FILE
              value: /shared/overlay-client.log
            - name: REVOCATIONS_FILE
              value: /state/revocations.json
            - name: PROVISIONS_FILE
              value: /state/provisions.json
            - name: ADMIN_PUBKEY_FILE
              value: /state/admin-pubkey
            - name: SEALED_ADMIN_KEY_FILE
              value: /state/admin-key-sealed.json
            # Per-node toggles set from the admin panel (IPv6, DHT, public
            # relays, rendezvous servers) persist here. WITHOUT this the panel
            # can display those switches but every save is refused with "no
            # persistent settings path configured on this node" — the endpoint
            # deliberately will not accept a setting it cannot remember across
            # a pod restart. easy-compose.yml has always set it; this file did
            # not, so the Kubernetes node was the one place the switches could
            # not be changed at all.
            - name: NODE_SETTINGS_FILE
              value: /state/node-settings.json
            # Admission control: "0" = WARN ONLY. The client build currently in
            # your registry may be the intermediate one that ENFORCED admission
            # unconditionally, which black-holes every unapproved peer while
            # control frames keep flowing — the node stays visible in peer lists
            # and passes no data. This pins it off regardless of which build runs.
            # Unapproved peers are logged once per peer per 30s:
            #   [admission] would drop <path> from unapproved peer <fingerprint>
            - name: ADMISSION_ENFORCE
              value: "0"
          ports:
            # The overlay transport. MUST match udp_listen_port in client.yaml.
            #
            # This hostPort is load-bearing in a way it was not under
            # hostNetwork: a pod namespace has no host-facing UDP port, so
            # without it nothing outside the node could ever punch back in — the
            # client could dial out and form outbound sessions, but every peer
            # trying to reach this node first would fail, and the podman node's
            # 127.0.0.1:6970 static peer would dial into nothing.
            - name: overlay-udp
              containerPort: 6970
              hostPort: 6970
              protocol: UDP
          volumeMounts:
            # Host /proc, read-only: the entrypoint reads the NODE's LAN addresses
            # from it (LAN candidates). Needs the container's existing privileged.
            - name: host-proc
              mountPath: /host/proc
              readOnly: true
            - name: shared
              mountPath: /shared
            - name: state
              mountPath: /state
            - name: client-config
              mountPath: /config
            - name: tun
              mountPath: /dev/net/tun
          resources:
            requests: {cpu: "50m", memory: "64Mi"}
            limits: {cpu: "2", memory: "512Mi"}
        # --- APGO admin dashboard --------------------------------------------
        - name: overlay-admin
          image: localhost:5000/apgo-admin:latest
          # IfNotPresent + a moving :latest tag means this node keeps whatever
          # it cached first. The dashboard HTML is embedded in this binary, so
          # if a dashboard change has not appeared, this is why — see "ROLLING
          # OUT A NEW BUILD" at the top of this file for the three commands.
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
          envFrom:
            # optional: if missing, you're prompted to create a dashboard login
            # on first visit.
            - secretRef:
                name: apgo-admin
                optional: true
          env:
            # 8789 (not 8788) so it can coexist with an old standalone APGO
            # stack still holding 8788 on the host during migration.
            - name: ADMIN_LISTEN
              value: ":8789"
            - name: CONTROL_SOCKET
              value: /shared/control.sock
            - name: LOG_FILE
              value: /shared/overlay-client.log
            - name: ADMIN_CREDS_FILE
              value: /adminkey/credentials.json
            - name: ADMIN_KEY_FILE
              value: /adminkey/admin.key
          ports:
            # Must match ADMIN_LISTEN (:8789). No hostPort: this is reached at
            # 10.22.22.22:8789 over the overlay, and in-cluster via the
            # apgo-admin Service below.
            - name: dashboard
              containerPort: 8789
          volumeMounts:
            - name: shared
              mountPath: /shared
            - name: state
              mountPath: /adminkey
              subPath: adminkey
          resources:
            requests: {cpu: "10m", memory: "32Mi"}
            limits: {cpu: "500m", memory: "256Mi"}
        # --- NGINX Proxy Manager ---------------------------------------------
        # Now a container in THIS pod rather than a Deployment of its own. Its
        # Node backend on :3000 is still fully private — a pod namespace hides
        # it from the node exactly as well as a separate pod did, so the "API is
        # not healthy" port-3000 collision cannot come back.
        #
        # Its route to overlay backends (10.22.22.x proxy hosts) is now this
        # pod's OWN ovl0, at the correct 1280 MTU. That is why the TCPMSS clamp
        # initContainer that used to live here is gone: nginx now negotiates MSS
        # against the real overlay path instead of against the CNI bridge.
        - name: nginx
          image: docker.io/jc21/nginx-proxy-manager:latest
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
          env:
            # Bind the admin UI (and proxy) on IPv4 only. On a host with a
            # non-routable IPv6 address the browser prefers [::]:81, loads the
            # page shell, then hangs on the /api calls — the classic "UI loads
            # but spins forever." Forcing IPv4 avoids that. (APGO's own overlay
            # IPv6 transport is unaffected — this only changes NPM's listeners.)
            - name: DISABLE_IPV6
              value: "true"
            # NOTE: the nginx "hang after a while on long-lived upstreams" fix is
            # NOT an env var — jc21/nginx-proxy-manager has no PROXY_REQUEST_BUFFERING
            # setting (it was silently ignored, so nginx kept buffering and kept
            # hanging). It's done the supported way, via the npm-custom
            # ConfigMap copied onto the PVC by the initContainer above.
          ports:
            # Public ingress. hostPort is correct for these: traffic arrives on
            # the node's physical NIC, which is the ordinary north-south path
            # portmap handles well. It was only TUN-ingress that it couldn't.
            - name: http
              containerPort: 80
              hostPort: 80
            - name: https
              containerPort: 443
              hostPort: 443
            # Admin UI — NO hostPort, by design. It is reachable at
            # 10.22.22.22:81 over the overlay because nginx binds it in the same
            # namespace as ovl0. Adding a hostPort here would not just be
            # redundant, it would install a PREROUTING DNAT rule that runs
            # BEFORE local socket lookup and would start intercepting overlay
            # traffic destined for this very socket.
            - name: npm-admin
              containerPort: 81
          # Give the Node backend + DB migrations time to finish on first boot
          # (slow on a Longhorn PVC) BEFORE liveness can fire. Without a startup
          # gate a first-boot migration that ran past the old 60s liveness delay
          # was killed mid-migration and the pod could never come up — the
          # "worked once, then crash-looped forever" symptom. While the startup
          # probe is running, liveness/readiness are suppressed.
          #
          # ── WHY exec AND NOT httpGet ────────────────────────────────────────
          # All three probes below run curl INSIDE the container against
          # 127.0.0.1 instead of letting the kubelet dial the pod IP. This is
          # not a style preference, it is the fix for an outage:
          #
          # An httpGet probe is dialled BY THE KUBELET, from the node, at the
          # pod IP. On this cluster that address is an overlay address (the
          # node network runs over APGO), so a plain health check on a socket
          # sitting inside this pod became dependent on overlay routing being
          # correct. On Aug 18 it wasn't: NPM answered 127.0.0.1:81/api/ in
          # under a second with {"status":"OK"} while the kubelet's probe to
          # 10.22.22.3:81 timed out 25 times in a row. Pod stuck at 2/3
          # Running, 0 restarts, no crash, nothing in the logs — because
          # nothing was wrong with NPM. Note the tell: a TIMEOUT rather than
          # "connection refused" means the SYN never reached local socket
          # lookup, i.e. the problem is the address, not the port.
          #
          # exec probes have no network path to break. They also keep working
          # unchanged if this pod is ever moved back to hostNetwork, where the
          # pod IP becomes the node IP and the same trap reopens.
          #
          # curl IS present in jc21/nginx-proxy-manager (verified: 8.14.1).
          # Keep each -m below the surrounding timeoutSeconds so curl exits on
          # its own and the kubelet reports a clean failure instead of a kill.
          #
          # Timings: the Node backend takes well over 300s on a cold Longhorn
          # PVC (migrations, certbot plugin install, IP-range fetches), so the
          # startup budget is 120 x 10s = 20 min. Once the startup probe
          # succeeds once it is never re-evaluated until the container
          # restarts.
          startupProbe:
            exec:
              command:
                - /bin/sh
                - -c
                - curl -fsS -m 8 -o /dev/null http://127.0.0.1:81/api/
            periodSeconds: 10
            timeoutSeconds: 10
            failureThreshold: 120  # up to 20 min on cold PVC / first boot
          # Surface backend health instead of silently serving a dead UI:
          # /api answers {"status":"OK"} only once the Node backend is up and
          # migrations have run. If it ever regresses, the pod goes unready —
          # visible in `kubectl get pods` instead of a mystery browser error.
          readinessProbe:
            exec:
              command:
                - /bin/sh
                - -c
                - curl -fsS -m 4 -o /dev/null http://127.0.0.1:81/api/
            periodSeconds: 10
            timeoutSeconds: 5
            failureThreshold: 3
          # Liveness must tolerate a momentarily-busy nginx. The default 1s
          # timeout meant one slow response — all workers briefly busy on
          # long-lived upstream streams — made the admin port miss a probe and
          # the kubelet KILLED the pod, turning a transient hang into a hard
          # crash-loop. 5s timeout + 6 failures rides out blips and restarts
          # only on a genuine, sustained (~2 min) hang.
          # Probe /api/ (not /): / is only the static UI shell and keeps
          # answering 200 even after the Node backend has died — so the
          # dashboard "works then stops," serving 502s on every /api call
          # FOREVER with no restart. /api/ returns 200 only while the backend is
          # actually alive, so a dead backend now self-heals via a restart.
          #
          # IMPORTANT: this probe restarts the nginx CONTAINER only, not the
          # pod. The overlay client keeps running and ovl0 survives, so an NPM
          # restart no longer disturbs overlay sessions — strictly better
          # isolation than the previous split, where the two were separate pods
          # but an apgo pod restart still dropped everything.
          livenessProbe:
            exec:
              command:
                - /bin/sh
                - -c
                - curl -fsS -m 4 -o /dev/null http://127.0.0.1:81/api/
            periodSeconds: 20
            timeoutSeconds: 5
            failureThreshold: 6
          # NPM had no requests/limits, making it BestEffort QoS — the FIRST
          # pod the kubelet OOM-evicts under node memory pressure, and with
          # nothing guaranteed it often didn't recover. Guarantee a floor and
          # cap the ceiling. Raised 512Mi -> 1Gi: OpenResty + the Node backend +
          # SQLite sit ~150-300 MB idle, but under a burst of long-lived proxied
          # connections the pod crept past 512Mi and was OOM-killed mid-serve —
          # the other half of the "works for a bit then stops" symptom.
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: "50m"
              memory: "256Mi"
            limits:
              cpu: "1"
              memory: "1Gi"
          volumeMounts:
            - name: npm-data
              mountPath: /data
            - name: npm-letsencrypt
              mountPath: /etc/letsencrypt
            # NOTE: the custom snippet (npm-custom ConfigMap) is NOT mounted
            # here — the install-custom-conf initContainer copies it onto the
            # npm-data PVC as a regular file. A direct ConfigMap mount is
            # read-only, which made NPM's startup chown fail and s6 kill the
            # container on every boot.
      volumes:
        - name: host-proc
          hostPath:
            path: /proc
            type: Directory
        - name: shared
          emptyDir: {}
        - name: state
          persistentVolumeClaim:
            claimName: apgo-state-v2
        - name: client-config
          configMap:
            name: apgo-config
            items:
              - key: client.yaml
                path: client.yaml
        - name: tun
          hostPath:
            path: /dev/net/tun
            type: CharDevice
        - name: npm-data
          persistentVolumeClaim:
            claimName: npm-data-v2
        - name: npm-letsencrypt
          persistentVolumeClaim:
            claimName: npm-letsencrypt-v2
        - name: npm-custom
          configMap:
            name: npm-custom
---
# APGO dashboard reachable in-cluster at apgo-admin.apgo.svc:8788 (the Service
# keeps port 8788 outward; the container listens on 8789).
#
# NOTE: NPM no longer needs this — it is a container in the same pod, so its
# proxy hosts can point at 127.0.0.1:8789 again. Kept for anything ELSE in the
# cluster that wants the dashboard.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: apgo-admin
  namespace: apgo
spec:
  selector:
    app: apgo
  ports:
    - name: dashboard
      port: 8788
      # The admin container listens on 8789 (ADMIN_LISTEN); 8788 stays the
      # in-cluster Service port.
      targetPort: 8789
---
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RENDEZVOUS — HTTPS discovery server (alternative to BitTorrent trackers)
#
# Nodes POST their network id + public endpoint here and get back the other
# endpoints. It never sees keys or the PSK, so it cannot join or decrypt the
# overlay — but it does see WHICH endpoints announce, so it runs authenticated.
#
# Credentials come from the apgo-rendezvous Secret. With OpenBao, project the
# secret with your usual mechanism (VSO / External Secrets / injector
# annotations) so it lands as a Secret named apgo-rendezvous with these keys:
#
#   AUTH_TOKENS   comma-separated bearer tokens        (e.g. "tok1,tok2")
#   AUTH_USERS    comma-separated user:password pairs  (e.g. "phone:pw1")
#
# Either key may be omitted; if BOTH are missing the server runs OPEN and says
# so in its log. Manual (non-OpenBao) creation for a quick test:
#
#   kubectl -n apgo create secret generic apgo-rendezvous \
#     --from-literal=AUTH_TOKENS="$(openssl rand -hex 24)"
#
# Clients then set that same value: desktop/mobile Settings → "Rendezvous
# username or token", or RENDEZVOUS_TOKEN / RENDEZVOUS_AUTH env on a headless
# node. Publish this Service through NPM on 443 with a real certificate — the
# credential is sent in an Authorization header, so TLS is what protects it.
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: apgo-rendezvous
  namespace: apgo
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: apgo-rendezvous
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: apgo-rendezvous
    spec:
      priorityClassName: apgo-critical
      # SAME PLACEMENT RULE AS THE apgo POD. Prefer zx3t/zx7t whenever either
      # can take it, but stay schedulable anywhere if both are gone.
      #
      # THE TOLERATIONS BELOW ARE WHAT MAKE THIS PREFERENCE MEAN ANYTHING.
      # zx3t and zx7t are control-plane nodes and carry
      # node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule. Without tolerating
      # it, this pod could NEVER land there, and the affinity would be a
      # preference for two nodes it is forbidden from using — silently, with no
      # error, just always running somewhere else. That was the state of this
      # Deployment before: no affinity AND no tolerations, so rendezvous only
      # ever ran on zx11t/zx5t while the apgo pod it serves ran on zx3t/zx7t.
      #
      # WHAT "PREFERRED" ACTUALLY GUARANTEES, because it is weaker than it
      # looks: weight 100 is the maximum for node affinity, but it is one SCORE
      # among several the scheduler sums — resource fit, image locality, taint
      # scoring. A zx3t/zx7t running near capacity CAN lose to an idle zx11t on
      # the resource-fit score even with the maximum affinity weight. This
      # cluster runs those two nodes close to their CPU requests, so that is a
      # live possibility rather than a theoretical one.
      #
      # Kubernetes has no manifest-level way to say "hard prefer, with
      # fallback". If placement on zx3t/zx7t ever needs to be a guarantee
      # rather than a strong hint, the levers are (a) raise the NodeAffinity
      # plugin weight in the scheduler profile — cluster config, not this file
      # — or (b) a PreferNoSchedule taint on zx11t/zx5t, which penalises them
      # for every pod, not just this one. A required affinity is NOT the
      # answer: that is what produced "0/4 nodes are available" and no fallback
      # at all.
      affinity:
        nodeAffinity:
          #
          # ORDER (2026-10-08): zx7t first, then zx3t, then any other node in
          # the required list. The weights add per node, so zx7t scores 100,
          # zx3t 60, every other node 0. Weight is a strong bias, not an absolute rule:
          # if zx7t cannot take the pod (cordoned, NotReady, hostPort or
          # resources taken) the scheduler falls through to zx3t, then zx11t.
          preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
            - weight: 100
              preference:
                matchExpressions:
                  - key: kubernetes.io/hostname
                    operator: In
                    values: ["zx7t"]
            - weight: 60
              preference:
                matchExpressions:
                  - key: kubernetes.io/hostname
                    operator: In
                    values: ["zx3t"]
      tolerations:
        # Lets zx3t / zx7t be schedulable at all — see above.
        - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoSchedule
        - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/master
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoSchedule
        # Move immediately when a node goes away. Safe to be this aggressive
        # here in a way it is not for stateful pods: rendezvous is stateless
        # with no PVC, so there is no RWO volume to strand on a dead node and
        # nothing to lose by starting a replacement early. Its whole state is
        # an in-memory endpoint map rebuilt by the next announce round.
        - key: node.kubernetes.io/unreachable
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoExecute
          tolerationSeconds: 0
        - key: node.kubernetes.io/not-ready
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoExecute
          tolerationSeconds: 0
      containers:
        - name: rendezvous
          image: localhost:5000/apgo-rendezvous:latest
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
          env:
            - name: LISTEN_ADDR
              value: ":8080"
            - name: PEER_TTL_SECONDS
              value: "300"
          envFrom:
            # optional: absent secret = open server (it logs a warning).
            - secretRef:
                name: apgo-rendezvous
                optional: true
          ports:
            - name: http
              containerPort: 8080
          # Stateless and unprivileged — the whole service is one in-memory
          # map of endpoint -> last-seen, rebuilt by the next announce round.
          securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
            capabilities:
              drop: ["ALL"]
          resources:
            requests: {cpu: "5m", memory: "16Mi"}
            limits: {cpu: "200m", memory: "64Mi"}
          # httpGet is FINE here, unlike on the apgo pod: this is an ordinary
          # CNI pod whose pod IP is a plain 10.42.x.y address with no overlay
          # involvement, so the kubelet's dial cannot be broken by a routing
          # fault in the overlay.
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8080}
            initialDelaySeconds: 5
            periodSeconds: 20
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8080}
            initialDelaySeconds: 2
            periodSeconds: 10
---
# Rendezvous reachable in-cluster at apgo-rendezvous.apgo.svc:8080.
# Point an NPM proxy host (443, with a certificate) at this Service, then give
# nodes the public URL, e.g. https://rv.example.com
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: apgo-rendezvous
  namespace: apgo
spec:
  selector:
    app: apgo-rendezvous
  ports:
    - name: http
      port: 8080
      targetPort: 8080
---
# NPM admin UI reachable in-cluster at npm-admin.apgo.svc:81.
#
# This is NOT the overlay path — 10.22.22.22:81 works directly now, because
# nginx binds :81 in the same namespace as ovl0. This Service exists only for
# other in-cluster workloads that want the NPM API.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: npm-admin
  namespace: apgo
spec:
  selector:
    app: apgo
  ports:
    - name: admin
      port: 81
      targetPort: 81
---
# Pre-pull DaemonSet — keeps every image of the apgo/npm stack warm on BOTH
# ingress nodes, so a failover of the apgo pod to the standby node never stalls
# on (or fails against) the registry. Each container pulls its image and then
# sleeps ~forever at zero cost; a rollout restart of this DaemonSet re-pulls
# after you push new :latest builds.
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: apgo-prepull
  namespace: apgo
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: apgo-prepull
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: apgo-prepull
    spec:
      # NO node affinity, deliberately. This DaemonSet exists so a failover
      # never waits on an image pull — which only helps on nodes the workload
      # can actually land on. Now that the Deployment's affinity is a
      # preference rather than a requirement, that is every node; pinning the
      # prepull to two of them would guarantee a cold pull on exactly the
      # occasion it was built to prevent.
      tolerations:
        - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoSchedule
        - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/master
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoSchedule
      containers:
        - name: apgo-client
          image: localhost:5000/apgo-client:latest
          imagePullPolicy: Always
          command: ["sleep", "infinity"]
          resources:
            requests: {cpu: "1m", memory: "8Mi"}
            limits: {cpu: "10m", memory: "32Mi"}
        - name: apgo-admin
          image: localhost:5000/apgo-admin:latest
          imagePullPolicy: Always
          command: ["sleep", "infinity"]
          resources:
            requests: {cpu: "1m", memory: "8Mi"}
            limits: {cpu: "10m", memory: "32Mi"}
        - name: npm
          image: docker.io/jc21/nginx-proxy-manager:latest
          imagePullPolicy: Always
          command: ["sleep", "infinity"]
          resources:
            requests: {cpu: "1m", memory: "8Mi"}
            limits: {cpu: "10m", memory: "32Mi"}
        # Keep the natpin image warm too, so the port pin is re-armed
        # immediately on a node that just came back rather than waiting on an
        # external registry at exactly the wrong moment.
        #
        # !! THIS MUST BE THE SAME IMAGE THE apgo-natpin DAEMONSET RUNS. !!
        # It was not, from 2026-09-04 to 2026-09-07, and that is the whole bug:
        # natpin moved from alpine:latest to debian-iptables and this entry was
        # left behind. So the image natpin actually needs was never warmed on
        # any node, IfNotPresent found nothing, and natpin sat in
        # ImagePullBackOff on zx5t/zx7t/zx11t for 27 HOURS — every surviving
        # node relaying, silently, which is exactly the failure the natpin
        # DaemonSet exists to prevent.
        #
        # Worse, prepull reported 4/4 Running the entire time, because it was
        # successfully warming an image nothing uses. A prepull entry that does
        # not match its consumer is not a warm cache, it is a false green light.
        #
        # IF YOU RETAG natpin, RETAG IT HERE IN THE SAME COMMIT.
        - name: natpin
          image: localhost:5000/apgo-natpin:latest
          imagePullPolicy: Always
          command: ["sleep", "infinity"]
          resources:
            requests: {cpu: "1m", memory: "8Mi"}
            limits: {cpu: "10m", memory: "32Mi"}
---
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# apgo-natpin — the reason this stack does not need hostNetwork
#
# THE PROBLEM. In a pod namespace, outbound overlay UDP is masqueraded by the
# CNI on the way out of the node, and the masquerade picks a DIFFERENT external
# source port for every destination. To a peer that is textbook SYMMETRIC NAT:
# the endpoint it learns from a tracker or from STUN is not the endpoint the
# next packet arrives from, so a coordinated hole punch can never line up and
# the session degrades to RELAY. Phones suffer worst — a tunnel provider's own
# sockets bypass its tunnel by OS design, so they cannot use the in-overlay
# fallback path that desktops can.
#
# Historically the answer was hostNetwork, which removes the CNI masquerade
# entirely. It also removes the namespace that keeps :3000, :81 and ovl0 out of
# the node's way, and it puts this client's TUN in the same routing table as
# the podman backbone client's. That trade has now failed twice, in two
# different ways (port collision on :3000; the kubelet losing its route to the
# probe target). See the header of this file.
#
# THE FIX. Keep the namespace and pin the port instead. One rule per node,
# inserted at the TOP of nat/POSTROUTING so it runs before the CNI masquerade:
#
#   -t nat -I POSTROUTING 1 -p udp -s 10.42.0.0/16 --sport 6970 \
#          -j MASQUERADE --to-ports 6970
#
# --to-ports forces the external port to 6970 for every flow, which makes the
# mapping ENDPOINT-INDEPENDENT: every peer observes the same
# <node public ip>:6970, matching what advertise_port announces and what the
# hostPort DNAT accepts inbound. That is a full-cone-like mapping, which is
# exactly the property hole punching requires.
#
# WHY IT IS SAFE. The match is narrow — UDP only, from the pod CIDR only,
# source port 6970 only. Nothing else on the node matches it. Reply traffic for
# inbound (hostPort-DNAT'd) sessions is handled by conntrack and never
# re-consults the nat table, so this rule cannot affect it. Many concurrent
# peers can share external port 6970 because a NAT tuple is
# (src, sport, dst, dport) — only the source half is pinned.
#
# WHY A DAEMONSET AND NOT A ONE-OFF COMMAND. The rule does not survive a reboot,
# and CNI restarts reorder POSTROUTING. The loop re-asserts it every 30s and
# removes it cleanly on shutdown. It runs on EVERY node for the same reason
# apgo-prepull does: the apgo Deployment's node affinity is a preference, not a
# requirement, so the pod can land anywhere and the pin has to already be there
# when it does.
#
# IF IT IS NOT WORKING, the pod says so: the readinessProbe re-checks that the
# rule is actually present, so `kubectl -n apgo get pods` shows natpin NotReady
# rather than silently leaving you relayed. Check its log first — the most
# likely cause is the iptables backend, below.
#
#   kubectl -n apgo logs ds/apgo-natpin --tail=20
#
# STILL REQUIRED, once per ingress node:  sudo ufw allow 6970/udp
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: apgo-natpin
  namespace: apgo
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: apgo-natpin
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: apgo-natpin
    spec:
      # hostNetwork here is correct and is NOT the thing this file warns about:
      # this container creates no TUN and binds no port. It needs the host's
      # network namespace only so its iptables writes land in the host's nat
      # table, which is where the CNI masquerade it must precede also lives.
      hostNetwork: true
      dnsPolicy: ClusterFirstWithHostNet
      priorityClassName: apgo-critical
      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 10
      tolerations:
        - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoSchedule
        - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/master
          operator: Exists
          effect: NoSchedule
      containers:
        - name: natpin
          # IMAGE MUST SHIP iptables. Built in-house from apgo-natpin.containerfile
          # (alpine:3.22 + iptables, both backends) and served from localhost:5000 like
          # the other apgo images. Changed 2026-10-07 from
          # registry.k8s.io/build-image/debian-iptables:bookworm-v1.0.4 — that
          # tag never existed upstream, so natpin sat in ImagePullBackOff on
          # every node and the port pin was absent cluster-wide (everything
          # to this pod relayed).
          #
          # Earlier history: changed from alpine:latest 2026-09-04.
          #
          # alpine has no iptables, so this container used to `apk add` it at
          # startup. That made a DaemonSet which is ITSELF PART OF THE NETWORK
          # PATH depend on outbound network before it can do its job — a
          # bootstrap circularity that fails silently per node. On 2026-09-04
          # zx11t and zx5t sat 0/1 Running for a day:
          #   [natpin] WARN: apk add iptables failed (no network?)
          #   [natpin] FATAL: no iptables available. Staying NotReady deliberately.
          # NAT pinning was silently absent on half the cluster, which degrades
          # peers on those nodes to RELAY — the exact failure this DaemonSet
          # exists to prevent, presenting as Running.
          #
          # debian-iptables is maintained upstream for exactly this job and
          # ships BOTH backends, so the nft/legacy detection below still works.
          # If you re-tag, verify the binaries survive:
          #   docker run --rm <image> sh -c 'iptables-nft -V; iptables-legacy -V'
          # A self-built image in the local registry is equally fine and the
          # apgo-prepull DaemonSet already warms images on every node.
          image: localhost:5000/apgo-natpin:latest
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
          securityContext:
            privileged: true
            capabilities:
              add: ["NET_ADMIN"]
          env:
            # Must match the cluster's pod CIDR (k3s default is 10.42.0.0/16;
            # confirm with `kubectl get node <n> -o jsonpath='{.spec.podCIDR}'`).
            # Too narrow and the rule never matches; too wide and it would pin
            # unrelated UDP that happened to use source port 6970.
            - name: POD_CIDR
              value: "10.42.0.0/16"
            # MUST equal udp_listen_port / advertise_port / the hostPort.
            - name: OVERLAY_PORT
              value: "6970"
            - name: NODE_NAME
              valueFrom:
                fieldRef:
                  fieldPath: spec.nodeName
          command: ["/bin/sh", "-c"]
          args:
            - |
              set -u

              CHAIN="POSTROUTING"
              RULE="-p udp -s ${POD_CIDR} --sport ${OVERLAY_PORT} -j MASQUERADE --to-ports ${OVERLAY_PORT}"

              # iptables comes FROM THE IMAGE now — nothing is installed at
              # runtime. This check is a backstop against a bad image tag, not
              # a recovery path: there is nothing to retry, so fail loudly and
              # stay NotReady rather than run without the rule.
              if ! command -v iptables-nft >/dev/null 2>&1; then
                echo "[natpin] FATAL: no iptables available. Staying NotReady deliberately."
                while true; do sleep 300 & wait $!; done
              fi

              # Pick the backend the HOST actually uses. A k3s node running
              # nft with our rule written into the legacy table gives you a
              # rule that exists, reads correctly, and never fires — strictly
              # worse than no rule, because it looks like it worked. Count the
              # nat rules in each and take the populated one (same heuristic
              # kube-proxy and calico use).
              NFT=$(iptables-nft-save -t nat 2>/dev/null | grep -c '^-' || true)
              LEG=$(iptables-legacy-save -t nat 2>/dev/null | grep -c '^-' || true)
              NFT=${NFT:-0}; LEG=${LEG:-0}
              if [ "$NFT" -ge "$LEG" ]; then IPT=iptables-nft; else IPT=iptables-legacy; fi
              echo "[natpin] node=${NODE_NAME:-?} backend=$IPT (nat rules: nft=$NFT legacy=$LEG)"

              # The readinessProbe runs this, so what the probe checks and what
              # the loop installs can never drift apart.
              printf '#!/bin/sh\nexec %s -t nat -C %s %s\n' "$IPT" "$CHAIN" "$RULE" > /tmp/check.sh
              chmod +x /tmp/check.sh

              cleanup() {
                echo "[natpin] removing rule on shutdown"
                $IPT -t nat -D $CHAIN $RULE 2>/dev/null || true
                exit 0
              }
              trap cleanup TERM INT

              while true; do
                if ! $IPT -t nat -C $CHAIN $RULE 2>/dev/null; then
                  if $IPT -t nat -I $CHAIN 1 $RULE 2>/dev/null; then
                    echo "[natpin] installed: -t nat -I $CHAIN 1 $RULE"
                  else
                    echo "[natpin] ERROR: could not install rule"
                  fi
                fi
                # Backgrounded so the TERM trap fires immediately on shutdown
                # instead of waiting out the sleep.
                sleep 30 & wait $!
              done
          readinessProbe:
            exec:
              command: ["/tmp/check.sh"]
            initialDelaySeconds: 20
            periodSeconds: 30
            failureThreshold: 2
          resources:
            requests: {cpu: "1m", memory: "16Mi"}
            limits: {cpu: "100m", memory: "64Mi"}

